<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Colony of Niger</h1><p class="st-meta">1900 CE – 1958 CE · capital Niamey</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The French territory in the central Sahel and southern Sahara, ruled first as a military territory, which became independent as Niger in 1960.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The French conquest of the area began with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voulet-chanoine-mission">Voulet-Chanoine mission</a> of 1899, notorious for massacring villagers, and the occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zinder">Zinder</a>, capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-damagaram">Sultanate of Damagaram</a>. In 1900 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> created a military territory, with headquarters first at Zinder. The territory became a colony of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa</a> in 1922, and the capital moved definitively to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niamey">Niamey</a> in 1926.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>French rule was thinly spread over a vast territory of farmers in the south and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toubou-people">Toubou</a> herders in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. Tuareg resistance culminated in the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaocen-ag-mohammed">Kaocen</a>, who besieged <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-agadez">Agadez</a> in 1916-1917. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundnut">Groundnuts</a> became the main cash crop, taxes and forced labour drove migration to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, and education reached very few.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After 1946 political parties emerged. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djibo-bakary">Djibo Bakary's</a> Sawaba party campaigned for a no vote in 1958, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamani-diori">Hamani Diori</a>, backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, prevailed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> became an autonomous republic in December 1958 and independent on 3 August 1960, with Diori as president.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/colony-of-niger.jpg" alt="Colony of Niger" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_France.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1900 CE – 1958 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Niamey</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Djibo Bakary, Hamani Diori</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Hausa, Zarma, Tamasheq</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/niger">Niger</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>French Niger, Military Territory of Niger</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1929&amp;focus=colony-of-niger" aria-label="Colony of Niger on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4738 1674 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Colony of Niger lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5058 1930L5217 1803M5058 1930L5195 1931M5058 1930L5174 1951M5058 1930L5054 728M5058 1930L4523 1888"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5217 1803h0M5195 1931h0M5174 1951h0M5054 728h0M4523 1888h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5058 1930h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5058 1930h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Niamey. <a href="/atlas?year=1929&amp;focus=colony-of-niger">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
